---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
import Header from '../components/Header.tsx'
import '../index.css'
---

<Layout title="About - PhyloGuessr">
  <div class="game">
    <Header client:load />
    <div class="about-page">
      <h2>About PhyloGuessr</h2>

      <h3>Data sources</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Taxonomy from <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/taxonomy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCBI Taxonomy</a></li>
        <li>Images from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikipedia</a></li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Contribute</h3>
      <p><a href="https://github.com/cmdcolin/phyloguessr" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a> · <a href="https://github.com/cmdcolin/phyloguessr/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Report bugs or suggest trios</a> · <a href="/donate">Donate</a></p>
      <h3>FAQ</h3>

      <h4>How do I play?</h4>
      <p>Pick which two of three organisms are most closely related — the pair sharing the most recent common ancestor.</p>

      <h4>How does scoring work?</h4>
      <p>In standard mode, you score points for a correct answer (reduced by 50% if you used a hint).</p>
      <p>In "closest pair" mode (6 species), your score reflects how close your chosen pair's common ancestor is relative to all other possible pairs. Closeness is measured by taxonomic rank — genus is closer than family, which is closer than order, and so on. For pairs whose common ancestor has an informal rank (like a clade node in NCBI Taxonomy), scoring uses the nearest formally-ranked ancestor above it, with tree depth as a tiebreaker within the same rank.</p>

      <h4>Is the taxonomy always accurate?</h4>
      <p>NCBI Taxonomy is a classification, not a true phylogeny — it draws from multiple sources and may not reflect the latest phylogenetic understanding. See the <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK53759/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCBI Taxonomy Help</a> for details.</p>

      <h4>Why does phylogeny matter?</h4>
      <p>Phylogeny has applications across medicine, agriculture, conservation, forensics, and more. See the <a href="/why">Why it matters</a> page for examples and a guide to model organisms.</p>

      <h4>Where did the idea come from?</h4>
      <p>I attended a Science on Tap trivia night where they played this type of game live — it was entertaining, and I thought it would make a fun web game. The name borrows the "-guessr" format made iconic by <a href="https://www.geoguessr.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GeoGuessr</a>.</p>

      <h4>Can I use this for my own trivia night or classroom?</h4>
      <p>Absolutely! Feel free to use it for trivia, teaching, or anything else.</p>


    </div>

    <h3>Use of AI</h3>
    <p>I used Claude Code to help build this website and even to suggest ideas for species comparisons in the 'easy' scenarios and fun facts. If you find any issues feel free to report to <a href="https://github.com/cmdcolin/phyloguessr/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a> or email <a href="mailto:colin.diesh@gmail.com">colin.diesh@gmail.com</a>.</p>


  </div>
</Layout>
